f67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d2a8b3b9-b92f-5d1f-96e6-db3a14c320c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79458/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "3beb8cef-4fe7-5d0f-ad24-43527c1021b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79458/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e4fb5069-0f28-5454-ad98-782d2f4bac5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79458/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-79458/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-79458/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-79458/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-79458/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-79458/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79458" :question/id #uuid "15b01c05-a846-58d7-9fe9-046ca9485817" :question/math-academy-id "q-79458" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the fourth quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$\n- $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $\\frac{3}{2}-\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{3}{2},-1)$, which lies in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and the imaginary part $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2})$, which lies in the first quadrant.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035975/assets/d7390ddcc8d338d2fd46bc12b0a4c65f8e6bf6e8c4f6c9fa802e2acea159416b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-79458/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79458"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-69899/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "ede556e2-fb3a-5636-856f-d24dd4da63da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-69899/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "9830ee31-d229-5e50-a59b-c72a74309405" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-69899/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "166682f6-ab26-5f5f-812f-14c3bc9a0882" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-69899/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "66064b9a-caec-5ba3-809b-0368cce45795" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-69899/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "df4c7f88-aafe-50bc-9d63-942e9f9951f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-69899/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0be00c72-62e9-5718-858c-6102d0b60075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-69899/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-69899/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-69899/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-69899/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-69899/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-69899/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-69899" :question/id #uuid "5f599a30-a12e-5fb2-b8d4-552e44102ddc" :question/math-academy-id "q-69899" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $-2+\\text{i}$\n- $-3-2\\text{i}$\n- $-3+2\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035975/assets/d683b808bee4b7893952adbaf87940066f9ff7d61c99ce7cc7e05fc9a4fbe02f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-69899/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-69899"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5034"] :question/problem "What complex number is represented in the Argand diagram below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035975/assets/79285ea5c9dc4b535b972f40fb16c9deaf57d895b2081eb789d74663525e12d2.png)" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the point are $(-2,3)$.\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-2,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-2+3\\textrm{i}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5035"] :question/problem "What complex number is represented in the Argand diagram below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035975/assets/7bcf26c8df340bc61e1863043ba2126dea07661c2e288fa5321ddd5ae9e67b46.png)" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the point are $(-4,0).$\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=-4,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the number depicted is $z=-4+0\\textrm{i},$ which can be simplified to $z= -4.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5036"] :question/worked-solution "We are given the complex number $z=4+\\textrm{i}$.\n\n- The real part of this complex number is $x=4,$ and\n\n- the imaginary part is $y=1.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the point are $(4,1)$, as shown in the Argand diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035975/assets/3ec41e707260b15e36da91542b98a66abcaa7950a3d4b06a95f919a204aec797.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
